  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\n- Showers Possible through South, Patchy Dense Fog Friday morning\n \nAnother MCS is moving from the west. This system is moving slower\nthan yesterday's, but like the last few days the pattern and axis\nof convection will keep showers and storms primarily south of\nMichigan. There is a slight chance that the fringes of the MCS\nclips south of the I 94 corridor. \n With the overall pattern remaining in place, the light winds and\nmoisture at the surface will once allow for fog Friday morning.\n Winds will be out of the north to northeast so slightly less\nmoisture so while there will be fog, it should not be as dense as\nThursday morning. \n\n- Showers/storms Friday night into Sunday; Heavy Rain possible Saturday\n\n The pattern begins to change Friday. Another ribbon of moisture \nand front will swing down Friday. Any convection from that should\nwill be similar as the last few days, only clipping far southern\nMichigan. \n However, the flow will shift to the north Friday night into\nSaturday. This will bring warm air advection, and a strong 850mb\njet through the region Saturday. \n That jet, along with potential mid level shortwaves, will bring a\nchance for strong to severe storms Saturday. Anomalous moisture\nassociated with this system will also mean heavy rainfall will be\npossible Saturday through Saturday night. \n As such, SPC has MI in a marginal risk, south of the I 96\ncorridor. There is also a slight risk of excessive rainfall\nthrough that region as well. \n Another wave associated with a 500 mb trough and a deepening\nupper level low will move through the UPPER Great Lakes Sunday.\nThis will bring further chances for showers and storms Sunday.\n \n\n- Dry to begin next week, chances for precipitation midweek\n\n Temperatures are trending near normal, to slightly below normal. \nA mid level ridge in the wave of the trough will allow for dry\nweather through the first half of next week. \n Another upper level low moving through central Canada is tracking\na long wave trough through the area Wednesday. Mid to long range\nmodels are fairly consistent at this time so that could allow \nfor showers and storms mid next week.",
